Hangs under BIT_GC=stress, and the hang is this program's own subject matter.

schedpreempt (#2582, epic #1768) exists to prove the runtime has NO scheduler
preemption: it runs a compute-bound green task that never yields and never
reaches a safepoint. The collector's stop-the-world rendezvous waits for every
task to arrive at one. A task that never yields never arrives, so a collection
that begins while it is running cannot complete. BIT_GC=stress collects
aggressively enough that this is not a race but a certainty.

MEASURED AS A HANG, NOT AS CONTENTION, and the discriminator is the point.
The harness's own INCONCLUSIVE verdict asks for exactly this check -- "re-run
the named program(s) standalone on a quiet host to tell them apart" -- so it
was run standalone:

  in the 0.1.26 pre-push batch:  TIMED OUT after 300s, host load average 8.64
  standalone, quiet box:         TIMED OUT after 300s, host load average 3.59

Load 3.59 on 18 cores is idle. A program that misses a 300s deadline there is
not losing a race for CPU. Both runs are BIT_GC=stress; the default-policy run
passes in the same batch (`0 failed`), which is what localises the fault to the
stress policy rather than to the program.

WHAT THIS WAIVER DOES NOT COVER. The default-policy run still executes the
program and still has to match schedpreempt.expected, so the thing #2582 was
written to demonstrate is still demonstrated on every gate run. Only the
stress-policy mask is waived, and the BIT_GC=off mirror run must still collect
exactly zero.

REMOVING THIS MARKER NEEDS PREEMPTION, not the closing of a bug. While a
compute-bound task can starve the rendezvous, any program of this shape will
hang under BIT_GC=stress -- so the marker comes out when #1768 lands scheduler
preemption and a task can be interrupted at a safepoint it did not ask for.
Do not delete it because some individual ticket closed.

Second-order, and the reason this is worth a marker rather than a revert: this
is the first program in the corpus to actually exercise a non-yielding task
under an aggressive collector. The property it uncovered -- that one such task
wedges collection process-wide -- was true before #2582 and nothing ran into it.
Reverting the program would return the corpus to not knowing.
